- {"summary": "The paper introduces NAIT, a framework for neuron-aware data selection in instruction tuning of large language models (LLMs). NAIT evaluates IT data by analyzing neuron activation patterns to enhance specific domain capabilities. Experiments show NAIT's efficiency, robustness, and interpretability, outperforming existing methods using only 10% of the data.", "significance_and_novelty": "NAIT presents a novel approach by leveraging neuron activation patterns for data selection, offering a new paradigm in instruction tuning for LLMs.", "reasons_for_acceptance": ["NAIT demonstrates significant performance improvements using only 10% of the data, as shown in Table 2.", "The method is efficient, reducing computational costs compared to existing methods (Section 5.2).", "NAIT provides interpretability by analyzing neuron activation patterns (Section 5.3).", "The framework shows strong cross-task transferability of neuron activation features (Section 5.3)."], "reasons_for_rejection": ["The paper lacks detailed theoretical analysis of the neuron activation patterns (Section 3.1).", "The empirical evaluation, while extensive, could benefit from more diverse datasets beyond Alpaca-GPT4 (Section 4.1)."], "suggestions": ["Include a theoretical analysis of neuron activation patterns to strengthen the framework's foundation.", "Expand empirical evaluations to include more diverse datasets to validate NAIT's generalizability."], "rating": 6, "confidence": 4, "soundness": 3, "presentation": 3, "contribution": 3}

- {"summary": "The paper introduces NAIT, a method for selecting high-quality instruction tuning data for LLMs by analyzing neuron activation patterns. NAIT aims to enhance model performance by aligning IT data with target domain capabilities. Experiments show NAIT outperforms existing methods, improving performance with less data.", "significance_and_novelty": "NAIT offers a novel approach by using neuron activation patterns for data selection, which is a significant departure from traditional methods relying on surface-level similarity.", "reasons_for_acceptance": ["NAIT demonstrates significant performance improvements over existing methods, as shown in Section 5.1.", "The method is robust across different tasks and models, as detailed in Section 5.1.", "NAIT provides a cost-efficient solution by using only 10% of the original IT data, as highlighted in Section 5.2."], "reasons_for_rejection": ["The paper lacks clarity in explaining the technical details of neuron activation feature extraction, as noted in Section 5.", "The scalability of NAIT to models larger than 20 billion parameters is untested, as mentioned in the limitations."], "suggestions": ["Clarify the technical process of neuron activation feature extraction in Section 5.", "Explore scalability to larger models beyond 20 billion parameters in future work."], "rating": 6, "confidence": 4, "soundness": 3, "presentation": 2, "contribution": 3}

- {"summary": "The paper introduces NAIT, a novel instruction data selection framework based on neuron activation patterns, aimed at enhancing model capabilities in reasoning and general tasks. It claims improvements in mathematical reasoning, multilingual understanding, and coding ability, and provides a cross-task neuron feature library and the Alpaca-NAIT dataset.", "significance_and_novelty": "NAIT is the first framework using neuron activation patterns for instruction data selection, offering a new paradigm for model capability development.", "reasons_for_acceptance": ["NAIT introduces a novel approach using neuron activation patterns for data selection, which is a significant contribution.", "The paper provides empirical evidence of performance improvements in complex tasks like mathematical reasoning and multilingual understanding.", "The open-sourcing of the cross-task neuron feature library and Alpaca-NAIT dataset adds value to the research community."], "reasons_for_rejection": ["The paper lacks detailed theoretical analysis of why neuron activation patterns are effective for data selection (Section 1).", "The empirical evaluation, while showing improvements, does not compare NAIT against a wide range of existing methods (Section 4.2)."], "suggestions": ["Include a more comprehensive comparison with existing methods in the empirical evaluation.", "Provide a deeper theoretical analysis of the effectiveness of neuron activation patterns in data selection."], "rating": 6, "confidence": 4, "soundness": 3, "presentation": 3, "contribution": 3}
